senseglove-api-dev for Sparky Stereo OS
=======================================

What is here: the headers of SenseGlove's C++ API under /usr/include/SenseGlove/ (Core and Connect), a CMake package in
/usr/lib/cmake/SenseGlove/, and SenseGlove's example in /usr/share/doc/senseglove-api-dev/examples/ with a CMakeLists.txt.
They are SenseGlove B.V.'s files under the MIT licence (LICENSE.SenseGlove), taken from github.com/Adjuvo/SenseGlove-API at the
commit named in SOURCE and checked against sources.tsv when the package was built.

What is not here: libsgcore.so and libsgconnect.so. SenseGlove publishes them only as binaries, so the edition cannot build them
and does not carry them. They arrive when you ask for them: open Stereo APTus, VR and SteamVR, "SenseGlove API libraries (C++)".
The entry shows the size (2.8 MiB) and the address first, fetches the two files from SenseGlove's repository at the same commit,
checks each against its SHA-256, and puts them in /opt/senseglove-api/lib. Until then find_package(SenseGlove) fails and says so.
Your own copy works too: cmake -DSENSEGLOVE_LIBRARY_DIR=DIR (or the environment variable of the same name).

Build the example:

  cp -r /usr/share/doc/senseglove-api-dev/examples /tmp/sgexample && cd /tmp/sgexample
  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ && cmake --build build

The libraries were built with LLVM's libc++, not with libstdc++ (their exported names say std::__1::vector and std::__1::basic_string; v20, v21
and v22 alike), so a program that uses them must be built with clang++ and -stdlib=libc++: apt install clang libc++-dev libc++abi-dev.
Built with g++ the link fails with undefined references such as SGCore::HapticGlove::ToString[abi:cxx11](SGCore::EHapticLocation).
The CMake targets add -stdlib=libc++ for Clang and refuse another compiler with this reason. Pass no std::string or std::vector
between such a program and a library built with libstdc++.

On Linux, SenseCom (the other Stereo APTus entry in VR and SteamVR) connects the gloves; the API talks to it.
The example is SenseGlove's Windows example: it waits for a key with system("pause"), which does not exist on Linux, so
without a glove it prints its message again and again instead of waiting.
